'<svg viewBox="0 0 640 360" role="img"><rect x="60" y="90" width="240" height="120" rx="14" fill="#000" opacity="0.8"/><text x="180" y="150" text-anchor="middle" font-size="42" font-weight="800" fill="#ffee58" class="pop">1729</text><text x="180" y="186" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" fill="var(--dg-sub)">the famous TAXI number</text><text x="120" y="250" font-size="17" fill="var(--dg-txt)">1729 = 1³ + 12³</text><text x="120" y="284" font-size="17" fill="var(--dg-txt)">1729 = 9³ + 10³</text><text x="120" y="322" font-size="15" fill="var(--dg-sub)">(two ways to build it as two cubes!)</text><text x="440" y="250" font-size="16" fill="var(--dg-txt)">Hardy called it dull.</text><text x="440" y="280" font-size="16" fill="var(--dg-txt)">Ramanujan smiled:</text><text x="440" y="310" font-size="16" fill="var(--dg-txt)">"It is a very interesting number!"</text><text x="320" y="40" text-anchor="middle" font-size="21" font-weight="800" fill="var(--dg-txt)">Srinivasa Ramanujan: number whisperer</text><text x="440" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" fill="var(--dg-sub)">no college degree, only a slate and chalk,</text><text x="440" y="144" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" fill="var(--dg-sub)">yet his formulas still solve problems</text><text x="440" y="168" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" fill="var(--dg-sub)">in black holes and string theory today</text></svg>',
