const CHAOS={
intro:'🌀 PROFESSOR CHAOS is un-teaching the universe! One by one, stolen science concepts are vanishing from the world. You are the last Science Detective of the Gurukool. Restore every concept — learn it, prove it, lock it back into reality!',
taunts:['🌀 Chaos cackles: “Nice guess… but WRONG! Care to try the lesson again, detective?”','🌀 Chaos sneers: “That concept STAYS stolen unless you learn it properly!”','🌀 Chaos laughs: “Ooh, so close! Read the case file again…”'],
cheer:['🌀 Chaos gasps: “Impossible! You are restoring it…!”','🌀 Chaos shrieks: “STOP solving my unsolvable puzzles!”','🌀 Chaos grumbles: “Lucky guess. It will not happen twice!”'],
tales:{
physics:{h:'Chaos erased GRAVITY and MOTION! Balls hang mid-air and nothing falls anywhere. Restore the laws that make things move!',c:'Next rumour: birds are falling UP somewhere in the sky worlds…'},
flight:{h:'Chaos stole FLIGHT! Every aircraft is frozen mid-sky. Wings have forgotten what lift is — teach them again!',c:'A silent shadow glides past the moonless night… something is up there.'},
chem:{h:'Chaos scrambled MATTER itself! Sugar tastes sour and metals float like foam. Chemistry must be re-learned before dinner dissolves!',c:'A strange liquid is bubbling in the kitchen… and it is NOT soup.'},
bio:{h:'Chaos silenced LIFE! Plants refuse to grow and cells have forgotten their jobs. Bring the living world back!',c:'Something tiny just moved in the soil sample. Something ALIVE.'},
logic:{h:'Chaos stole THINKING ITSELF! Puzzles have no answers and patterns have gone random. Only sharp reasoning can stitch logic back!',c:'The next puzzle box carries a note: “Solvable? We shall see…”'},
elec:{h:'Chaos cut THE CURRENT! The whole city is dark — not one electron will march. Re-teach electricity or sit in the dark forever!',c:'A spark flickers far away… a generator? Or a trap?'},
light:{h:'Chaos bent LIGHT the wrong way! Rainbows are grey and mirrors show nothing. Restore reflection, refraction and colour!',c:'A shadow just moved where nothing stood…'},
heat:{h:'Chaos stole HEAT FLOW! Ice stays frozen in fire and tea never warms. Temperature itself is on strike!',c:'Something cold is breathing at the window… in summer.'},
body:{h:'Chaos hijacked THE HUMAN FACTORY! Hearts skip, bones soften, nerves tangle. The body’s blueprints must be rebuilt!',c:'Your own pulse just echoed TWICE. Investigate the body worlds again soon…'},
earth:{h:'Chaos froze THE EARTH’S ENGINE! Clouds stand still, rivers forget to flow, seasons stop mid-turn. Restart the planet!',c:'The ground beneath the school trembled for exactly one second today…'},
ai:{h:'Chaos scrambled THE NETWORKS! The internet forgets its own pathways and AIs guess nonsense. Debug the digital brain!',c:'A garbled message arrives: “h3lp m3 1 am st1ll l3arning…”'},
materials:{h:'Chaos weakened EVERY MATERIAL! Bridges sag, glass bends, cloth tears like paper. Re-engineer the stuff of the world!',c:'A paper-thin wall held your weight today. Suspicious…'},
sky:{h:'Chaos blacked out THE SKY! Constellations scattered, the Pole Star hides. Re-map the heavens!',c:'A new star just winked… where no star should be.'},
scientists:{h:'Chaos erased THE GREAT SCIENTISTS from history! Their discoveries still work — but nobody remembers why. Restore the legends!',c:'A dusty diary page appears, signed only “1729?!”'},
kitchen:{h:'Chaos poisoned THE KITCHEN SCIENCE! Cakes fall flat, curd never sets, tea forgets to steep. Take back the tasty physics and chemistry!',c:'Tonight, the fridge hums a strange new tune…'},
coding:{h:'Chaos broke THE CODE! Every program loops forever and variables swap values at random. Think like a debugger!',c:'A screen blinks: ERROR 404 — LOGIC NOT FOUND.'},
robotics:{h:'Chaos rewired THE ROBOTS! Sensors lie and motors wander. A drone circles the lab, singing backwards. Rebuild robot logic!',c:'Beep… boop… “m3ssag3 from Ch40s: nic3 try.”'},
quantum:{h:'Chaos collapsed REALITY’S BASEMENT! The quantum realm leaks: coins spin as heads AND tails, particles whisper across rooms. Face the strangest world of all!',c:'Your shadow just split in two. Only for a second. Probably.'},
genetics:{h:'Chaos cut THE DNA CODE! The recipe book of life has missing pages. Splice the story of inheritance back together!',c:'A mysterious twin-word appears in your notes: you have never written it. Or have you?'},
vedic:{h:'Chaos stole ANCIENT WISDOM! India’s greatest discoveries — zero, surgery, astronomy — are fading from memory. Restore the rishis’ science!',c:'An old verse glows faintly: it hides a number. Find it.'},
trivia:{h:'Chaos dares you to a RAPID-FIRE DUEL! Everything you have restored, jumbled into 10 wild questions. Survive the arena!',c:'Chaos himself awaits at the final gate… one day.'},
acid:{h:'Chaos switched SOUR AND SLIPPERY! Soap stings like lemon and lime feels like lotion. The pH ladder lies in pieces!',c:'A red cabbage in the garden turned ALL seven colours at once…'},
float:{h:'Chaos overturned BUOYANCY! Steel ships dive and wooden balls sink like stones. The water forgot how to carry!',c:'A paper boat just sailed UP a waterfall. IMPOSSIBLE…'},
isro:{h:'Chaos grounded THE MISSIONS! Rockets freeze on the pad and satellites drift blind. India’s space story must be re-flown!',c:'Mission control just received a signal… from the WRONG direction.'},
cosmos:{h:'Chaos swallowed THE STARS! Constellations unravel and a black hole gapes where the North Star should be. Relight the universe!',c:'Light from a dead star still reaches you tonight… 500 years late.'},
eco:{h:'Chaos cut THE WEB OF LIFE! Predators graze, grass hunts, decomposers quit their jobs. Restitch every food chain!',c:'A single bee followed you home today. It knows something.'},
terra:{h:'Chaos stopped THE EARTH’S HEART! Plates jam, quakes sputter, volcanoes hiccup ash. Restart the restless planet!',c:'The school bell rang 11 times today. The ground counted them all…'},
optics:{h:'Chaos cracked THE LENSES! Eyes blur, cameras lie, microscopes sleep. Bend the light back into focus!',c:'Everything you saw in the mirror today was… upside down?'},
micro:{h:'Chaos weaponised THE MICRO WORLD! Friendly microbes fled and germs parade openly. Recruit your immune army!',c:'A single drop of pond water, left on your desk, is MOVING…'}},
restore:'✅ CONCEPT RESTORED! 🌀 Professor Chaos shrieks and retreats — “You have won THIS time, detective!” Reality stitched a little tighter.',
outro:'You restored the concept and earned the Chaos-Buster star. The universe thanks you, detective!'};
const LV2={
physics:[
{s:'A train leaves Pune at 08:00 and reaches Mumbai (150 km away) at 10:30, including a 30-minute halt at a station. A cyclist covers the same 150 km in 15 hours.',q:'What is the train’s average speed for the whole trip, and how many times faster than the cyclist is it?',o:['75 km/h · 7.5× faster','60 km/h · 6× faster','60 km/h · 15× faster','50 km/h · 5× faster'],a:1,w:'Average speed counts TOTAL time (2.5 h) including the halt: 150 ÷ 2.5 = 60 km/h. Cyclist: 150 ÷ 15 = 10 km/h, so 6× faster. The trap: forgetting the halt!'},{s:'Ball A is dropped from rest; Ball B is thrown horizontally from the same height at the same moment.',q:'Which ball lands first (ignore air)?',o:['A, because B’s sideways motion slows its fall','Both land together — vertical fall is independent of horizontal motion','B, because it moves faster','Cannot be determined'],a:1,w:'Gravity pulls both down equally; horizontal velocity doesn’t affect vertical acceleration. A classic olympiad separation-of-axes result.'},
{s:'A 60 N crate is pushed 5 m across a floor with a 10 N horizontal force, then lifted 2 m at constant speed.',q:'How much work does the pushing force do, and which force does work during the lift?',o:['300 J; gravity does the lifting work','50 J; the lifting force does 120 J against gravity','600 J; friction does the lifting','0 J; no work without acceleration'],a:1,w:'Work = force × distance in the force’s direction: push = 10 × 5 = 50 J. Lift: upward force = 60 N × 2 m = 120 J done by the lifter against gravity.'}],
flight:[
{s:'4 paper planes are tested. Plane W has wide wings, X narrow, Y swept-back, Z wide with a bent-up trailing edge. All launched identically; Z glides steadiest.',q:'What does Z’s bent-up edge most likely provide?',o:['Thrust','Extra lift and self-stability (a built-in tail-up trim)','Less weight','Better colour'],a:1,w:'Bending the trailing edge up increases the wing’s effective angle of attack and creates a restoring moment — the paper-plane version of a tail elevator. Stability beats raw lift in gliders.'},
{s:'A drone must hover perfectly still in a steady 20 km/h wind.',q:'What must it do relative to the air?',o:['Fly at 0 km/h through the air','Fly at 20 km/h exactly into the wind','Fly at 20 km/h with the wind','Hovering is impossible in wind'],a:1,w:'To stay over one ground point, it must cancel the wind: fly 20 km/h airspeed opposite to the wind, making ground speed zero. Velocity vectors must sum to zero!'},
{s:'A spacecraft in a circular orbit fires its engine briefly BACKWARD (against its motion).',q:'What happens to its orbit?',o:['It spirals down into a lower, faster orbit','It rises to a higher orbit','The orbit is unchanged','It stops immediately'],a:0,w:'Slowing drops it into a lower energy orbit — where it actually speeds UP (counter-intuitive olympiad favourite: braking = falling closer = moving faster, like descending a gravity well).'}],
chem:[
{s:'In a sealed bag, 18 g of water is split by electricity into hydrogen and oxygen gases. Later the gases are re-burned.',q:'What is the mass of water recovered, and why?',o:['Less than 18 g — some mass was destroyed','18 g — mass is conserved; the changes are just rearrangements','More than 18 g — burning creates matter','0 g — gases are massless'],a:1,w:'No atoms enter or leave the sealed system: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O returns exactly the starting atoms. Mass conservation during physical/chemical change.'},
{s:'Salt is stirred into water until no more dissolves, leaving crystals at the bottom. The mixture is filtered.',q:'How could you recover the dissolved salt from the clear filtrate?',o:['Freeze the water','Evaporate the water','Filter again with finer paper','Add more salt'],a:1,w:'Dissolved salt passes any filter; only evaporation (or boiling) separates it — crystallisation. Filtration catches only INSOLUBLE bits.'},
{s:'3 liquids are tested: P mixes with water and conducts; Q mixes but does not conduct; R separates into a layer.',q:'Which liquid is most likely a pure covalent substance like alcohol?',o:['P','Q','R','None'],a:1,w:'Alcohol dissolves in water (covalent molecular) but forms no ions, so it doesn’t conduct. P behaves like an acid/salt solution (ions); R is immiscible like oil.'}],
bio:[
{s:'A potted plant is kept in bright light but its soil is waterlogged for a week. The leaves turn yellow and droop even though water is plentiful.',q:'What is the most likely explanation?',o:['Too much light kills chlorophyll','Waterlogged soil lacks air spaces, so roots cannot respire and begin to rot','The plant is thirsty for mineral ions only','Leaves need darkness at night'],a:1,w:'Roots need oxygen for respiration. Waterlogging expels soil air → root failure → wilting despite water. A favourite trap: more water ≠ healthier plant.'},
{s:'A cell is 0.02 mm wide. Under a microscope the image measures 8 mm.',q:'What magnification is being used?',o:['40×','400×','4000×','0.4×'],a:1,w:'Magnification = image ÷ real = 8 mm ÷ 0.02 mm = 400×. A standard skills question: convert units (0.02 mm = 20 µm; 8000 µm ÷ 20 µm = 400).'},
{s:'2 identical plants are grown: one in full sun, one in a cupboard with a grow-light tuned to green-only light.',q:'Why does the green-light plant grow poorly?',o:['Plants are allergic to green','Chlorophyll reflects (hardly absorbs) green light, so little energy is captured','Green light is too weak','Cupboards block oxygen'],a:1,w:'Leaves LOOK green because chlorophyll reflects green — meaning that colour is barely used in photosynthesis. Appearance predicts absorption!'}],
logic:[
{s:'A clock reads 3:15. A student claims the hour and minute hands overlap.',q:'What is the exact angle between the hands?',o:['0°','7.5°','15°','30°'],a:1,w:'At 3:15 the minute hand is at 90°; the hour hand has moved 15/60 × 30° = 7.5° past 3 (90°). Angle = 90 − 97.5 = 7.5°. Classic olympiad clock reasoning.'},
{s:'A snail climbs a 10 m wall: up 3 m each day, down 2 m each night.',q:'On which day does it first reach the top?',o:['Day 8','Day 9','Day 10','Day 11'],a:1,w:'Net +1 m/day, but on day 9 it starts at 8 m and climbs 3 m to 11 m — reaching 10 m BEFORE the night slide. Days of “just multiply 10×1” end on day 10 — one day late!'},
{s:'In a class photo, Meena is 7th from the left end AND 7th from the right end of the row.',q:'How many students are standing in the row?',o:['13','14','12','15'],a:0,w:'7th from the left means 6 students stand before her; 7th from the right means 6 stand after her. Total = 6 + Meena + 6 = 13. The common trap is 14 — double-counting Meena from one end.'},
],
elec:[
{s:'Circuit data: Battery 6 V. Bulb P draws 0.5 A, bulb Q draws 2 A.',q:'Which bulb has higher resistance, and what is its value?',o:['P; 12 Ω','Q; 3 Ω','P; 3 Ω','Both equal; 6 Ω'],a:0,w:'R = V ÷ I. P: 6 ÷ 0.5 = 12 Ω. Q: 6 ÷ 2 = 3 Ω. Less current at the same voltage = more resistance.'},
{s:'3 identical bulbs are in SERIES. One is unscrewed and the other 2 go dark. The same 3 are rewired in PARALLEL and the same bulb is unscrewed.',q:'What happens in the parallel circuit, and why?',o:['Both go dark — circuits always need all bulbs','The 2 stay lit — each parallel branch is an independent path to the battery','They flicker','They glow dimmer but stay on'],a:1,w:'Parallel branches are independent loops; removing one leaves the others’ loops intact. Series has one shared path — one break kills all.'},
{s:'A fuse is a thin wire designed to melt and break the circuit if the current gets too high.',q:'Where must a fuse be placed, and what property makes it melt first?',o:['In the live wire path; high resistance + low melting point','Anywhere; made of copper','Across the battery; it stores charge','In the earth wire; it is magnetic'],a:0,w:'The fuse sits in the main current path and is deliberately thin/high-resistance with a low melting point — excess current heats it fastest, sacrificing itself to protect the circuit.'}],
light:[
{s:'A ray hits a plane mirror at 40° to the mirror SURFACE (not the normal).',q:'What is the angle of reflection (measured from the normal)?',o:['40°','50°','80°','20°'],a:1,w:'Angle from normal = 90° − 40° = 50°. Law of reflection: angle in = angle out, both measured from the normal. The surface-vs-normal trap catches most students!'},
{s:'A blue shirt is viewed under PURE red light in a dark room.',q:'What colour does the shirt appear?',o:['Blue','Red','Black','White'],a:2,w:'A blue shirt reflects only blue; red light contains no blue to reflect — almost nothing bounces back, so it looks black. Colour = the light an object REFLECTS, not what it “is”.'},
{s:'A periscope uses 2 parallel plane mirrors at 45°.',q:'Why does the final image appear upright and not left-right flipped awkwardly?',o:['One mirror flips the image and the second flips it back — 2 reflections cancel','Mirrors never flip images','The 45° angle is magic','Light slows inside the periscope'],a:0,w:'Each reflection inverts one axis; 2 parallel mirrors restore the image — a double reflection preserves handedness. Submarine periscopes depend on this symmetry.'}],
heat:[
{s:'Equal masses of water (4.2 J/g°C) and oil (2.0 J/g°C) receive the same heating for 1 minute, both starting at 20°C.',q:'Which statement is correct?',o:['Oil ends hotter — lower specific heat means each joule raises its temperature more','Water ends hotter','Both end at the same temperature','Oil does not warm at all'],a:0,w:'ΔT = Q ÷ (m × c). Smaller c (oil) → bigger temperature rise for the same heat input. That is why oil sizzles fast while water takes ages to boil.'},
{s:'A metal spoon feels colder than a wooden spoon in the same room, though a thermometer reads them identical.',q:'Why does the metal FEEL colder?',o:['Metal is actually at a lower temperature','Metal conducts heat away from your skin faster, draining warmth quicker','Wood generates its own heat','Thermometers cannot read metal'],a:1,w:'Both are at room temperature — but touch measures heat FLOW RATE, not temperature. Metal’s high conductivity pulls heat from your 37°C skin faster, triggering “cold”.'},
{s:'Sea breeze: land heats faster by day, and by night the wind reverses.',q:'Which explains the NIGHT-TIME land breeze?',o:['Land cools faster; cool dense air over land sinks and pushes out to the warmer sea','The sea gets colder than land at night','The Moon pulls air shoreward','Waves blow air inland'],a:0,w:'At night land cools quickly below sea temperature → air over the sea is warmer and rises → cool land air flows out to replace it. Same convection engine, reversed by differing heat capacities.'}],
body:[
{s:'A person’s left ventricle pumps 70 mL per beat at 72 beats per minute.',q:'Roughly how much blood is pumped per minute, and which chamber pumps to the lungs?',o:['About 5 L/min; the right ventricle','About 0.7 L/min; the left atrium','About 7 L/min; the left ventricle','About 50 L/min; the right atrium'],a:0,w:'70 mL × 72 ≈ 5,040 mL ≈ 5 L/min (cardiac output). The RIGHT ventricle sends blood to the lungs; the left ventricle (thicker-walled) sends it to the whole body.'},
{s:'A vial of ink is injected into a vein in the foot. A sensor detects it first in the heart, then lungs, then heart again.',q:'Which blood vessels carried it BETWEEN its 2 heart visits?',o:['Arteries to the lungs, pulmonary veins back','The aorta twice','The vena cava, then the coronary arteries','Capillaries in the brain'],a:0,w:'Route: body → vena cava → right heart → pulmonary ARTERY → lungs → pulmonary VEIN → left heart → aorta. Pulmonary arteries are unique: arteries carrying deoxygenated blood.'},
{s:'After a large meal, a student’s blood sugar rises, then falls back to normal within 2 hours.',q:'Which hormone brought it down, and what organ released it?',o:['Insulin; pancreas','Adrenaline; kidneys','Thyroxine; thyroid','Bile; liver'],a:0,w:'Insulin sweeps glucose into cells for storage, lowering blood sugar. Its partner glucagon raises it when low — a balancing pair, like a thermostat for sugar.'}],
earth:[
{s:'At 12 noon in Delhi (82.5°E), the local solar time in Kolkata (88.5°E) is later.',q:'How many minutes later does the Sun reach its highest point in Kolkata?',o:['24 minutes','6 minutes','48 minutes','12 minutes'],a:0,w:'Earth turns 15° per hour = 1° per 4 minutes. Kolkata is 6° east of Delhi → 6 × 4 = 24 minutes ahead. Longitude → time is an olympiad staple.'},
{s:'A storm cloud builds vertically into a towering anvil, then bursts into a thunderstorm with hail.',q:'Which sequence did the cloud go through?',o:['Evaporation → condensation → precipitation (with updrafts growing the cloud)','Condensation → evaporation → freezing','Precipitation → condensation → collection','Transpiration → sublimation → rain'],a:0,w:'Warm moist air rises (updraft), cools, condenses into droplets releasing latent heat that powers further rise — until drops/hail are too heavy: precipitation.'},
{s:'The same star is observed from a city on 1 January and again on 1 July — it rises about 2 hours earlier in July.',q:'What causes this drift?',o:['Earth’s orbit around the Sun changes the direction of night','The star is speeding up','Earth’s tilt wobbles the horizon','The atmosphere thickens in summer'],a:0,w:'Each day Earth moves ~1° along its orbit, so the stars rise ~4 min earlier daily — ~2 h per month. The star clock versus the Sun clock.'}],
ai:[
{s:'A spam filter is trained on 100 emails. After training it correctly flags 47 of 50 spam and 40 of 50 genuine emails.',q:'What are its 2 error rates, and what would MORE training data most likely improve?',o:['Misses 3 spam, false-flags 10 genuine; more data improves both','It is perfect','Misses 10 spam, flags 3 genuine; nothing improves with data','50% accuracy overall'],a:0,w:'Spam missed: 50−47 = 3; genuine flagged: 50−40 = 10. Bigger, varied training sets typically reduce BOTH error types — pattern coverage grows.'},
{s:'A program must decide if a fruit is “ripe” from colour (0–100) and softness (0–100). Ripe if colour > 70 AND softness > 60. Fruit X: colour 85, softness 50. Fruit Y: colour 65, softness 90.',q:'Which fruits does the rule classify as ripe?',o:['Neither','X only','Y only','Both'],a:0,w:'X fails softness (50 ≤ 60); Y fails colour (65 ≤ 70). AND requires both conditions — a single failed test rejects. This is how simple classifiers (and this question) work.'}],
materials:[
{s:'2 iron nails: one plain, one coated with grease, are left outdoors for a month. Only the plain one rusts.',q:'What did the grease do?',o:['Kept water and oxygen away from the iron surface','Chemically strengthened the iron','Made the nail slippery for rain','Absorbed sunlight'],a:0,w:'Rusting needs BOTH air and moisture. Grease is a barrier coating — same principle as paint, galvanising or oiling a bicycle chain.'},
{s:'A glass rod and a rubber rod are bent the same amount. The glass snaps; the rubber springs back.',q:'Which property difference explains this?',o:['Elasticity — rubber deforms and returns; glass is brittle','Rubber is heavier','Glass conducts heat better','Rubber is transparent'],a:0,w:'Brittle materials store little elastic energy and fracture; elastic materials return to shape. Shape-memory alloys push this to superhero levels.'}],
sky:[
{s:'On a clear night, a star is seen to rise exactly in the east and set exactly in the west.',q:'What can you conclude about this star?',o:['It lies on the celestial equator','It lies near the Pole Star','It is a planet','It must be a satellite'],a:0,w:'Only objects on the celestial equator rise due east and set due west for all observers. Stars further north or south rise at oblique angles.'},
{s:'The Moon shows the same face to Earth at all times, and completes one spin on its axis in the same time as one orbit.',q:'What is this lock-step called, and what causes it?',o:['Synchronous rotation, caused by Earth’s tidal pull on the Moon','Magnetic locking, caused by the Moon’s iron core','Solar wind pressure','Coincidence'],a:0,w:'Earth’s gravity braked the Moon’s spin over eons until spin period = orbit period — so one face stays hidden (the “far side”, not “dark side”).' }],
scientists:[
{s:'C.V. Raman’s experiment: blue light passing through a liquid emerges slightly changed in colour — some photons gave a bit of energy to the molecules themselves.',q:'What is the name of this scattering, and what does the shifted light reveal?',o:['Raman effect; it fingerprints the molecules’ vibrations','Photoelectric effect; it measures electron charge','Reflection; it reveals the mirror quality','Refraction; it measures density'],a:0,w:'The tiny wavelength shift encodes the molecule’s vibration modes — a molecular fingerprint that won Raman the Nobel and now powers chemistry labs everywhere.'},
{s:'Aristotle said heavy objects fall faster. Galileo disagreed but could not drop objects from the Moon.',q:'Which reasoning did Galileo use to challenge Aristotle?',o:['A thought experiment: 2 bricks tied together would have to fall both faster AND slower than one brick — contradiction','He asked astronauts','He weighed clouds','He trusted older books'],a:0,w:'The linked-bricks paradox: joined, they are heavier (should fall faster), yet the slower one should drag (should fall slower) — Aristotle’s rule contradicts itself. Pure logic beat authority.'}],
kitchen:[
{s:'2 identical eggs are boiled: one for 6 minutes in plain water, one for 6 minutes with salt added.',q:'What difference does the salt actually make?',o:['Raises the boiling point slightly, so the salted egg cooks at a marginally higher temperature','Cooks the egg faster by magic chemistry','Makes the egg bigger','No difference at all to anything'],a:0,w:'Dissolved salt elevates boiling point a little (≈0.5°C at kitchen concentrations) — a real but tiny effect. Trap: “no difference” is tempting; colligative effects ARE real.'},
{s:'Chapati dough is kneaded, then left covered for an hour. It puffs up with tiny bubbles.',q:'Which gas fills the bubbles, and which organism made it?',o:['Carbon dioxide; yeast','Oxygen; bacteria','Hydrogen; mould','Nitrogen; viruses'],a:0,w:'Yeast ferments sugars → CO₂ bubbles, leavening the dough. Same chemistry as idli batter and bread — biology you can eat.'},
{s:'Ice cubes with a frozen lemon slice melt faster on a plate than plain ice cubes of the same size.',q:'Which is the best explanation?',o:['Dark/absorptive inclusions absorb more radiant heat and create impurities that slightly lower melting efficiency','Lemon is hot inside','The plate dislikes lemon','Plain ice is heavier'],a:0,w:'Impurities and darker zones change absorption and the melting behaviour — small effects, but olympiad questions reward noticing that “identical” is never quite identical.'}],
coding:[
{s:'A robot repeats: “move 2 steps forward, turn left 90°” — 4 times, then stops. It started facing north.',q:'Where does it end relative to its start?',o:['8 steps north','Back at the start','8 steps east','4 steps north, 4 steps west'],a:0,w:'4 left turns make one full circle — headings cancel, but the forward moves add up: 2 × 4 = 8 steps in the ORIGINAL direction (north). Loops that turn can still translate you!'},
{s:'A program’s bug: “count” starts at 0 and adds 1 each loop, but prints 4 after looping 5 times.',q:'Which fix is correct?',o:['Initialise count at 1, or add 1 5 times starting from 0 and print 5 — the loop must run n times adding once each','Print count+4','Loop only 4 times','Delete the counter'],a:0,w:'The classic off-by-one: either the initial value or the loop count is wrong. Debugging = checking WHERE counting starts and WHERE it ends.'}],
robotics:[
{s:'A line-following robot uses 2 light sensors. Sensor readings: LEFT sees black (value 900), RIGHT sees white (value 100). The robot steers LEFT and rejoins the line.',q:'What does this tell you about the black line’s position and the control logic?',o:['The line drifted RIGHT; logic steers toward the sensor that sees dark','The line vanished; logic stops','Both sensors failed','The robot turns right'],a:0,w:'Black absorbs sensor light (high reading = dark). Right sensor sees white means the line is no longer under the RIGHT side — it slipped LEFT… careful: steering left worked, so the line was under the LEFT (dark) sensor: follow-the-dark logic.'},
{s:'A Mars rover must decide actions with a 20-minute signal delay to Earth.',q:'Why must it carry its own decision-making?',o:['Round-trip commands take ~40 minutes — it must react autonomously to survive hazards','It runs faster than radio','Earth is too far to see the Sun','Rovers cannot receive signals'],a:0,w:'Autonomy is survival when “stop!” takes 20 minutes to arrive. Sense → decide → act must happen locally — exactly why rover software is tested for years.'}],
quantum:[
{s:'A quantum computer’s qubit can be 0, 1, or a blend of both until measured.',q:'What happens when you READ the qubit, and why does this make copying impossible?',o:['It snaps to one definite value; reading disturbs the blend, so no perfect copy can be made','It doubles','Nothing; the blend persists','It becomes 2 qubits'],a:0,w:'Measurement collapses superposition — and the no-cloning theorem follows: to copy you must read, and reading destroys the original blend. Quantum secrets can’t be photocopied.'},
{s:'Schrödinger’s cat is famously BOTH alive-and-dead until the box opens.',q:'What is the serious point behind the joke?',o:['Quantum superpositions are real but fragile; interaction with the environment forces a single outcome','Cats are special','Boxes block physics','Dead and alive are the same thing'],a:0,w:'The thought experiment highlights the measurement problem: superposition exists at particle scale, but “looking” (any interaction with the environment) collapses it to one classical result.'}],
genetics:[
{s:'In guinea pigs, black (B) dominates brown (b). 2 black pigs have a brown baby.',q:'What are the parents’ genotypes, and what fraction of their offspring should be brown?',o:['Both Bb; one quarter','Both BB; none','One BB, one Bb; half','Both bb; all'],a:0,w:'A brown (bb) baby needs a b from EACH parent — so each black parent carries b: Bb × Bb. Punnett square gives bb in 1 of 4 boxes.'},
{s:'A DNA segment reads: ATG CCA GGT. mRNA copies it with U in place of T.',q:'What does the mRNA read?',o:['UAC GGU CCA','AUG CCA GGU','TAC GGT CCA','UAG CCU GGA'],a:1,w:'Transcription swaps T→U but keeps the same letters otherwise: AUG CCA GGU. (UAC GGU CCA would be the anti-parallel complement — the reverse-direction trap!)'}],
vedic:[
{s:'The katapayadi verse “gopibhagyamadhuvrata…” encodes π to 30 places: each consonant is a digit, read right-to-left.',q:'Why encode maths in poetry at all?',o:['Verses are memorable, checkable by chanting, and survive copying errors — memory insurance before printing','To keep maths secret from kings','Poems compute faster','Paper was forbidden'],a:0,w:'Oral tradition needed error-proof storage: meter and rhyme flagged mistakes, and beautiful verses stuck in memory. Data compression, 1600s Kerala edition.'},
{s:'Aryabhata stated Earth rotates daily and gave π ≈ 3.1416, centuries before heliocentrism debates in Europe.',q:'What does his work best demonstrate about the scientific method?',o:['Precise observation and mathematics can overturn “obvious” everyday impressions','Ancient texts are always right','Rotation was discovered in Europe','Mathematics cannot describe the sky'],a:0,w:'Everyone “saw” the sky turn; Aryabhata computed that Earth itself rotates — the courage to let calculation overrule common sense is the heart of science.'}],
acid:[
{s:'A mystery liquid turns red litmus blue AND turmeric paste red-brown. When mixed with an equal amount of another liquid, the mixture turns litmus NEITHER red nor blue.',q:'What was the second liquid, and what is the reaction called?',o:['An acid; neutralisation','A stronger base; amplification','Water; dilution','Salt solution; nothing happened'],a:0,w:'First liquid = base. Adding an acid in the right proportion makes salt + water — neutral, so litmus stays neutral-purple. Classic neutralisation.'},
{s:'An antacid tablet (base) weighs 0.5 g. It fully neutralises 25 mL of stomach acid. A stronger episode needs double the acid neutralised with the SAME tablet brand.',q:'What must change, and what stays constant?',o:['2 tablets needed; the acid stays hydrochloric','One tablet but drink more water','Half a tablet; acid type changes','No change possible'],a:0,w:'Neutralisation is stoichiometric: twice the acid needs twice the base. The acid is still HCl — only the AMOUNT changes.'},
{s:'A jeweller tests 3 identical-looking metals with dilute acid: Metal X fizzes with a colourless gas that pops with a lit splint; Metal Y does nothing; Metal Z fizzes with a gas that turns limewater milky.',q:'What are the 2 gases and which metal is likely a carbonate compound?',o:['Hydrogen and carbon dioxide; Z','Oxygen and hydrogen; Y','Carbon dioxide and oxygen; X','Hydrogen and nitrogen; Z'],a:0,w:'Pop-test gas = hydrogen (acid + reactive metal, X). Limewater-clouding gas = CO₂ — produced when acid attacks CARBONATES, so Z is a carbonate mineral, not a pure metal.'}],
float:[
{s:'A wooden block floats with 60% of its volume under water. A second block of the SAME wood floats in oil (density 0.8 g/cm³).',q:'What fraction of the second block sinks in oil?',o:['75%','60%','48%','80%'],a:0,w:'Floating fraction = liquid density ÷ object density. Wood density = 0.6 × 1 = 0.6 g/cm³. In oil: 0.6 ÷ 0.8 = 0.75 → 75% submerged. Same block, different liquid, different waterline!'},
{s:'An egg sinks in fresh water but floats in very salty water.',q:'What changed between the 2 liquids, and what is the general rule?',o:['Salt raises the liquid’s density; objects float when the liquid they displace outweighs them','Salt makes eggs lighter','Fresh water is heavier','Eggs dissolve in salt'],a:0,w:'Dissolved salt adds mass per cm³ — salty Dead Sea water (~1.24 g/cm³) buoys you high. Buoyancy compares object density with FLUID density.'},
{s:'A ship sails from a river into the open sea (saltier, denser water) carrying the same load.',q:'What happens to how deep the ship sits?',o:['It rides slightly HIGHER — denser water pushes harder for the same displaced volume','It sinks deeper','No change','It depends on the wind'],a:0,w:'Same weight needs less displaced volume in a denser liquid — the hull rises a little. Ships have load-line marks (“Plimsoll lines”) for exactly this fresh-vs-salt difference.'}],
isro:[
{s:'Chandrayaan-1 took about 15 days to cover ~3.84 lakh km to the Moon, riding a fuel-saving path with several loops of Earth first.',q:'Roughly what was its average speed in km/h, and why could the cruise be far below Earth’s 11.2 km/s escape speed?',o:['About 1,070 km/h — reaching the Moon is only a part-way climb out of Earth’s gravity, not a full escape','11.2 km/h — that IS escape speed','About 40,000 km/h — it braked constantly','About 100 km/h — spacecraft are slow'],a:0,w:'3,84,000 km ÷ (15 × 24 h) ≈ 1,070 km/h (≈ 0.3 km/s). Escaping Earth COMPLETELY needs 11.2 km/s, but the Moon sits part-way down our gravity well — clever multi-loop paths buy the climb cheaply.'},{s:'A satellite in a LOW orbit (160 km) circles Earth every ~90 minutes; a geostationary satellite (35,786 km) takes 24 hours.',q:'What general rule does this pair demonstrate?',o:['Closer orbits are FASTER — orbital speed and period fall with distance','Farther orbits are faster','Orbit speed depends on the satellite’s mass','All orbits take 90 minutes'],a:0,w:'Gravity weakens with distance: near Earth the pull is fierce, demanding ~7.8 km/s, while distant orbits crawl. Mass of the satellite is irrelevant — Galileo’s insight, orbital edition!'},
{s:'ISRO’s PSLV launched 104 satellites in one flight (2017) by releasing them into slightly different orbits one after another.',q:'Why not release them all at once?',o:['Each satellite needs its own velocity and direction — one big release would leave identical orbits (and collisions)','It looked better on TV','Rockets cannot open twice','To save battery'],a:0,w:'Orbit is set by release velocity and altitude. A staggered sequence with small burns places each payload on its own path — space traffic management before it was cool.'}],
cosmos:[
{s:'Star A is 4 light-years away; star B is 100 light-years away. A space telescope photographs both tonight.',q:'Which statement is true?',o:['The photo shows star A as it was 4 years ago, star B as it was a century ago','Both are shown as they are tonight','Star B no longer exists','Star A is older than star B'],a:0,w:'Light travels at a finite speed, so every glance into space is a look back in time — astronomers literally study the universe’s past by looking far away.'},
{s:'Our Sun will shine steadily for ~10 billion years; a blue supergiant burns out in just a few million.',q:'Why do bigger stars die younger?',o:['More mass → stronger gravity → faster fusion burning through fuel quickly','They start with less fuel','Big stars are colder','They orbit faster'],a:0,w:'Like a bonfire versus a candle: the giant’s fierce core pressure fuses fuel at a huge rate. Big = bright = brief. Our modest Sun gets the long life.'},
{s:'A black hole’s event horizon shrank when it merged with another, briefly shaking spacetime itself — detected in 2015 as LIGO’s chirp.',q:'What carried the energy of that shaking away?',o:['Gravitational waves — ripples in spacetime travelling at light speed','Sound waves through space','X-rays','Neutrinos only'],a:0,w:'LIGO heard 2 black holes merging over a billion light-years away via gravitational waves — a brand-new sense for astronomy, first predicted by Einstein a century earlier.'}],
eco:[
{s:'A food chain: grass → goat → tiger. Ecologists find 10,000 kg of grass in a reserve that could sustain 4 tigers at the 10% rule.',q:'Roughly how much goat biomass is needed, and what does this imply about tiger territory?',o:['~1,000 kg of goats; big predators need HUGE ranges to collect enough energy','100 kg of goats; tigers are small','10,000 kg of goats; tigers are efficient','Tigers eat grass in droughts'],a:0,w:'10,000 × 10% = 1,000 kg goat level → 100 kg tiger level (≈1 big tiger per ~2,500 kg of grass…). The 10% staircase forces predators to roam widely — the physics of being fierce and rare.'},
{s:'Wolves were returned to a park; deer moved around more, willows regrew along rivers, and even songbirds increased.',q:'What is this cascade of effects called?',o:['A trophic cascade — a keystone predator reshapes the whole web','Coincidental weather','Evolution in fast-forward','Overpopulation'],a:0,w:'One keystone link (wolves) rewired vegetation and rivers — the Yellowstone wolf story is ecology’s most famous ripple-effect proof.'},
{s:'Fungi in the soil pass nutrients to trees in exchange for tree-made sugars.',q:'What does this underground partnership suggest about “competition” in nature?',o:['Many relationships are mutual trades, not just competition','Fungi are parasites always','Trees are independent','Sugar is waste'],a:0,w:'The “wood wide web”: mycorrhizal networks trade minerals for sugar and even relay stress signals. Ecosystems run on both rivalry AND partnership.'}],
terra:[
{s:'Fossils of the SAME sea creature are found in matching rock layers in India, Africa and Antarctica.',q:'What is the strongest conclusion?',o:['These lands were once joined (Gondwana) — the creature swam one connected ocean','Someone carried fossils around','Creatures can fly between continents','Rock layers randomise'],a:0,w:'Identical fossils + matching-layer sequences across now-distant lands is the classic evidence for continental drift — the pieces of the jigsaw were once together.'},
{s:'P waves from a quake arrive at Station X but NO S waves arrive at all.',q:'What can be deduced about the material between the quake and the station?',o:['The waves crossed a LIQUID layer (the outer core) — S waves cannot travel through liquid','S waves are too slow to bother','The station is on the Moon','Nothing can be deduced'],a:0,w:'S (shear) waves need rigid material. Their disappearance on far-side paths proved Earth’s outer core is liquid — a whole layer diagnosed from missing wiggles!'},
{s:'A granite tombstone (igneous) in a rainy city has gone soft and crumbly in 200 years, while a 4,000-year-old granite temple pillar inland is sharp.',q:'Which factor most likely explains the difference in weathering rate?',o:['More water and pollution in the city accelerate chemical weathering','The temple was recently carved','Granstone comes in soft and hard types','Rain avoids temples'],a:0,w:'Weathering rates depend on climate and chemistry: moisture + acid rain dissolve minerals faster. Same rock, different environment, wildly different lifespan.'}],
optics:[
{s:'An object sits 2× the focal length from a convex lens.',q:'Where is the image, and what is its size compared to the object?',o:['Between f and 2f on the other side; same size','At the focus; huge','Beyond 2f; magnified','No image forms'],a:0,w:'At exactly 2f the object distance equals image distance and heights match. Move closer than 2f and the image grows beyond life-size — the projector’s trick.'},
{s:'A short-sighted boy sees near objects clearly but distant ones blurred; his friend has the opposite problem.',q:'Which lenses correct each?',o:['Short-sight: concave; long-sight: convex','Short-sight: convex; long-sight: concave','Both convex','Both concave'],a:0,w:'Short-sighted eyes focus images in FRONT of the retina — a diverging (concave) lens pushes focus back. Long-sight is the reverse. Opticians are applied lens-benders!'},
{s:'Endoscopes (tummy-cameras) pipe light through a glass FIBRE by bouncing it repeatedly inside at a shallow angle — it never escapes sideways.',q:'Which optical phenomenon traps the light?',o:['Total internal reflection','Dispersion','Diffraction','Polarisation'],a:0,w:'Beyond a critical angle, light hitting the glass-air boundary reflects almost perfectly back inward — the same physics that makes underwater surfaces look mirror-like.'}],
micro:[
{s:'Data from an outbreak: 40 people ate at a wedding; 30 ate the paneer, of whom 24 fell ill; of the 10 who skipped paneer, only 1 fell ill.',q:'What does the data suggest, and what is the missing control?',o:['The paneer is the likely source; comparing ill-vs-well among paneer eaters is the key step','Everyone was ill anyway','The hall is the cause','Nothing can be inferred'],a:0,w:'Attack rate among paneer eaters = 80%; among avoiders = 10%. That contrast — the heart of epidemiology — points to the paneer. Pasteur would approve of comparing groups, not guessing.'},
{s:'Penicillin kills bacteria by wrecking their cell walls; human cells have no cell walls.',q:'Why does this make penicillin a “safe” antibiotic, and why is it useless against flu?',o:['It targets a structure humans lack; flu is viral — no wall to attack','Penicillin is mild poison for everyone','Viruses are immune to all drugs','It only works on fungi'],a:0,w:'Selective toxicity: attack what the pathogen has and the host lacks. Viruses borrow OUR machinery, so there is no unique wall to hit — hence antivirals are a different, harder game.'},
{s:'A microbiologist heats a flask of broth, bends the neck into an S-curve, and leaves it open — no microbes grow for months. When the neck is snapped off, broth spoils in days.',q:'What did this experiment prove?',o:['Microbes come from the air (they don’t appear spontaneously) — the S-neck trapped dust and germs','Heat makes food immortal','Air is poison','Broth creates life on its own'],a:0,w:'Pasteur’s swan-neck flask demolished “spontaneous generation”: sealed-from-dust broth stayed sterile; exposed broth teemed. Open questions + clever apparatus = biology’s greatest mic drop.'}]
};

const PREREQ={quantum:['physics','chem'],isro:['physics','sky'],optics:['light'],micro:['bio','body'],terra:['earth'],eco:['bio'],acid:['chem'],float:['physics'],robotics:['coding','elec'],cosmos:['sky'],genetics:['bio'],kitchen:['chem'],light:['physics'],heat:['physics'],elec:['physics'],body:['bio'],ai:['logic'],coding:['logic']};

'use strict';
